message: Please cite the paper; this repository supplies its source and finite checks.
type: software
title: "A trace costs one quantifier: the exact complexity of perfect play in nonlocal games"
version: 1.1.0
date-released: '2026-11-06'
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.24022996"
license: [CC-BY-4.0, MIT, Apache-2.0]
authors: [{"family-names": "Douglas", "given-names": "Seth", "orcid": "https://orcid.org/0009-0007-4708-3252"}, {"family-names": "Mghirbi", "given-names": "Nidhal", "orcid": "https://orcid.org/0009-0005-6534-1118"}]
keywords: ["nonlocal games", "arithmetical hierarchy", "computability", "von Neumann algebras", "zero-error communication", "quantum information"]
abstract: "Given a finite nonlocal game, can the players win with certainty? We prove that the existence of a perfect normal spatial strategy is $\\Sigma^0_2$-complete, even when restricted to games of value one, so it cannot be decided even with an oracle for the halting problem. This is strictly harder than perfect play in the finite-dimensional and commuting-operator models, which are $\\Sigma^0_1$-complete and $\\Pi^0_1$-complete. Within the commuting model, the existence of a perfect normal strategy with a finite or semifinite von Neumann host is $\\Sigma^0_2$-complete, whereas for every fixed type $\\mathrm{III}_\\lambda$ subtype it has the same $\\Pi^0_1$-complete language as unrestricted commuting play. A trace thus costs one existential number quantifier: the hardness constructions force every perfect strategy to choose a finite clock period or a finite tree depth. The spatial classification persists when finite entanglement entropy is required, and it is formally verified in Lean, assuming only a special case of Tarski's decision theorem. We also prove $\\Sigma^0_2$-completeness for one-shot zero-error communication with normal entanglement, and construct a finite channel with zero-error codes of a given size every one of which needs infinite entanglement entropy.\n\nThis paper consolidates and supersedes our preprints Perfect play with spatial quantum strategies is Sigma^0_2-complete (doi:10.5281/zenodo.23910986), Perfect play has different arithmetical complexity in semifinite and type III algebras (doi:10.5281/zenodo.23925091), and One-shot zero-error communication with normal entanglement is Sigma^0_2-complete (doi:10.5281/zenodo.23924868). Theorem D and the completion of the semifinite-but-not-spatial difference classification are new here. The Π⁰₁-completeness of commuting perfection is known from Slofstra, JAMS 33 (2020), doi:10.1090/jams/929, and Mousavi-Nezhadi-Yuen, arXiv:2110.04651; Theorem B identifies its language with that of every fixed type-III subtype."
